[UTCTime "21:38:02"]
[ChapterMode "gamebook"]

{ Let's get started! }
1. e4 { White plays e4 and we could play the Aggressive French but instead follow the green 🟩 arrow ⬆. } { [%csl Ge5,Re6][%cal Ge7e5,Re7e6] } 1... e5 (1... e6 { The Aggressive French! https://lichess.org/study/9Ir9Jxlv }) 2. Nf3 { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gb8c6] } 2... Nc6 3. Bc4 { They play the SUPER popular Italian game. 😏 How can we crush it? (Hint: green arrow)😉 } { [%csl Gf5][%cal Gf7f5] } 3... f5 { The Rousseau Gambit! } 4. exf5 { Most played move (and a mistake). Kick the knight! } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Ge5e4] } 4... e4 5. Ng1 { We must stop Qh5+. } 5... Nf6 { Not the computer's favorite, but the simplest. Feel free to experiment with 5. ...d5 if you want! 😉 } { [%csl Rh5][%cal Rf6h5,Rd1h5] } 6. d3 { Played 50% of the time. "Get your pawn away so I can develop my knight!!!" } { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd7d5] } 6... d5 { Nope. 😏 } 7. Bb5 { Take our pawn back. } { [%csl Gf5][%cal Gc8f5] } 7... Bxf5 8. dxe4 { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gf5e4] } 8... Bxe4 { Ok so now... what does black have? Better development, piece activity, center control, and the initiative. What does white have? The move. -1.2. Great job! 😀 } *


